commit df2a2546705f50ca267037702a34cd63aa6e35a6
Author: ahuzmeza <32289581+ahuzmeza@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Mon Nov 2 18:24:06 2026 +0200

    Mimi: fix resetState keeping the end of the previous clip (#368)
    
    resetState reset the SEANet encoder and decoder and the transformer
    caches but not the 12.5 Hz downsample and upsample convs. After a
    streaming encode the downsample conv still holds the last 2 or 3
    encoder steps (40 ms each) of the clip and has its left padding marked
    as applied, so the next encodeStep after resetState starts from the
    previous clip's tail instead of the edge padding. With 3 left over,
    every frame of the new clip is shifted by one encoder step, and the
    encode has one frame more when the new clip's step count is odd too;
    with 2, only the first frame differs.
    
    Marvis encodes every reference this way (encodeChunked calls resetState,
    then encodeStep per chunk), for every text piece of every generate call,
    built-in voices included, so every reference encode after the first on
    the same model was affected. The Rust Mimi in kyutai-labs/moshi resets
    both convs in reset_state, moshi_mlx does it in reset_all, and
    PocketTTS's MimiAdapter.resetState here already does.
    
    Marvis v0.2's Mimi (kyutai/moshiko-pytorch-bf16), encodeChunked as in
    Marvis, Tests/media/conversational_fr.wav and conversational_a.wav
    encoded after another reference on the same instance, against a fresh
    instance: 0 of 86 and 0 of 165 frames equal before (3.9% and 1.6% of
    tokens, one extra frame), all equal after. Fresh encodes are unchanged.
    Decoded, the stale codes give the same speech 40 ms later (same SNR to
    the clip, same Whisper transcript), so the reference keeps its content
    but the codes the model is conditioned on change after the first call.
    With the reference cut to whole frames, as the Marvis partial-frame fix
    does, 85 of 86 and 164 of 165 frames equal before (the first frame
    differs), all equal after.
    
    The test encodes one clip through a tiny random Mimi, calls resetState,
    encodes a second clip and expects a fresh instance's codes.

