commit 5fd737ceece61478f13a77374f51b1db2441c1cc
Author: ahuzmeza <32289581+ahuzmeza@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Sun Nov 1 00:54:37 2026 +0200

    GLM-ASR: transcribe audio longer than 30 s in chunks (#365)
    
    The audio encoder keeps max_whisper_length / merge_factor positions (375, 30 s of
    audio) and drops the rest, and generate encoded the whole file in one pass. With a
    RoPE checkpoint such as mlx-community/GLM-ASR-Nano-2512-4bit the encoder runs over
    the full file and the merge cuts it, so anything after 30 s was lost without an
    error. Python mlx-audio has split GLM-ASR input into 30 s chunks since
    Blaizzy/mlx-audio@5c1cc10.
    
    Audio longer than the window is now split with splitAudioIntoChunks and each chunk
    is decoded by the existing generate, as FireRedASR2 does since #362. The splitter
    can move a cut up to its silence-search window past the nominal length, so chunks
    are nominally 28 s with a 2 s search and none passes the encoder window. maxTokens
    caps each chunk, as Qwen3-ASR does since #294. The result carries one segment per
    chunk; the stream yields each chunk's text. Audio that fits the window and mel
    input take the same path as before.
    
    GLM-ASR-Nano-2512-4bit, Release CLI, word error rate against the script: a 72 s
    English clip (macOS say, 242 words) 0.587 -> 0.000, main returning 100 words; an
    88 s clip of joined TTS clips (225 words) 0.658 -> 0.076. The streamed text equals
    the final text in both arms.
    
    longAudioChunksFitTheEncoderWindow checks that 70 s of audio with quiet points
    just past each 30 s mark splits into chunks within the encoder window, which a
    30 s split with the default search does not.

