commit c758bf27345d28215626166117a4486ce938248d
Author: ahuzmeza <32289581+ahuzmeza@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Sat Oct 31 19:52:57 2026 +0200

    Kokoro English G2P: fold accents instead of dropping the letters (#354)
    
    The English lexicon has ASCII keys only ("cafe", "naive"), and the BART
    fallback's grapheme set is ASCII, so a word like "café" missed the
    lexicon and reached the fallback with "é" as an unknown token: Kokoro
    said "caf" ("calf"), "naïve" came out as "knave", "jalapeño" as
    "jalapeo".
    
    The lexicon lookup now folds diacritics first, so "café" and "naïve"
    use their "cafe" and "naive" entries, and the fallback folds them before
    mapping graphemes ("jalapeño" -> "jalapeno"). ASCII text is unchanged.
    Letters that do not decompose (ß, æ, ø) are not affected.
    
    Limit: where the plain spelling is a different English word, the folded
    word takes that word's entry: "rosé" reads as "rose", "résumé" as the
    verb "resume", likewise pâté, exposé, saké. These were wrong before too
    (the fallback dropped the "é").
    
    Kokoro-82M-bf16, 6 sentences with accented words, transcribed with
    whisper-large-v3-turbo; target words heard: 2/9 -> 7/9 (the two left are
    "fiancé" heard as "fiancée" and "crème" as "cream").

