commit 011287db71eb2e7d80bd6a00f9920845bcae15da
Author: ahuzmeza <32289581+ahuzmeza@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Sat Oct 31 19:50:39 2026 +0200

    Whisper, CohereTranscribe: stream deltas that continue the text already sent (#351)
    
    The streaming loops in Whisper and CohereTranscribe decode every token so far and
    send the part after the text already sent, or the whole transcript when the old text
    is not a prefix of the new one. That happens in two ways:
    
    - A token can end partway through a character. Whisper's byte-level decoder
      (swift-transformers) shows the unfinished bytes as U+FFFD, and Cohere's byte-fallback
      SentencePiece decoder leaves out the whole unfinished byte run, characters already
      sent included; either way the old text is no longer a prefix.
    - A token can end between a letter and its combining mark (Devanagari, Bengali, Thai,
      emoji skin tones). Swift's `hasPrefix` compares whole characters, so "नमस" is not a
      prefix of "नमस्".
    
    Replaying the loop on openai/whisper-large-v3's tokenizer, one sentence per language:
    Hindi 37 characters stream as 579, Bengali 29 as 547, Thai 32 as 158, emoji 26 as
    102, Chinese 16 as 48; Arabic, Vietnamese and French are not affected. Live, with
    whisper-large-v3-turbo `--stream --language zh` on 9 short Chinese clips, 4 re-sent
    the transcript: 74, 87, 43 and 19 characters streamed for transcripts of 15, 22, 15
    and 15. With Cohere Transcribe (beshkenadze/cohere-transcribe-03-2026-mlx-fp16) on
    the same clips, 1 re-sent the transcript: 27 characters for 17.
    
    The delta now waits while the text ends in U+FFFD or is a cut-short copy of the text
    already sent, and compares by Unicode scalar; any other change still sends the whole transcript, as
    before. When generation stops, what is still held is sent. In the same replay
    every language now streams exactly its transcript, and live all 9 streams equal the
    final text with either model. The final transcript is unchanged.
    
    transcriptDeltasWaitForACharacterSplitAcrossTokens uses a byte-level vocabulary that
    splits "é" into two tokens (the old logic streams "Caf\u{FFFD}CaféC", this "CaféC")
    and a Hindi word whose tokens end between a letter and its mark, a byte-fallback
    SentencePiece vocabulary in which "中" vanishes when the first byte of "文" arrives,
    and checks the end-of-stream case.
    
    Co-authored-by: Lucas Newman <lucasnewman@me.com>

